  "text": "To find the least positive value of \\( t \\) such that the sequence \\(\\arccos (\\cos \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos t \\alpha)\\) forms an arithmetic progression, we need to reason step by step.\n\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   The sequence of angles \\(\\alpha, 3\\alpha, 11\\alpha, t\\alpha\\) will be valid modulo \\(2\\pi\\). This is because the cosine function is periodic with period \\(2\\pi\\). Therefore, we can simplify the problem by reducing the angles to their equivalent values in the interval \\([0, 2\\pi)\\).\n\n2. **Arithmetic Progression:**\n   For the sequence to be an arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms must be the same. Therefore, we need:\n   \\[\n   \\arccos (\\cos 3\\alpha) - \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha) = \\arccos (\\cos 11\\alpha) - \\arccos (\\cos 3\\alpha)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   2 \\arccos (\\cos 3\\alpha) = \\arccos (\\cos 11\\alpha) + \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha)\n   \\]\n   Let's denote \\(x = \\arccos (\\cos 3\\alpha)\\). Then the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   2x = \\arccos (\\cos 11\\alpha) + \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplifying Further:**\n   Using the identity \\(\\arccos (\\cos 3\\alpha) = \\pi - 3\\alpha\\) (since \\(\\cos (2\\pi - 3\\alpha) = \\cos 3\\alpha\\)), we get:\n   \\[\n   2(\\pi - 3\\alpha) = \\arccos (\\cos 11\\alpha) + \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further:\n   \\[\n   2\\pi - 6\\alpha = \\arccos (\\cos 11\\alpha) + \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha)\n   \\]\n   We also know that \\(\\arccos (\\cos 11\\alpha) = 2\\pi - 11\\alpha\\) (since \\(\\cos (2\\pi - 11\\alpha) = \\cos 11\\alpha\\)). Therefore:\n   \\[\n   2\\pi - 6\\alpha = (2\\pi - 11\\alpha) + \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   2\\pi - 6\\alpha = 2\\pi - 11\\alpha + \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2\\alpha = \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha)\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\arccos (\\cos \\alpha) = \\alpha\\) (if \\(0 < \\alpha < \\pi/2\\)), we get:\n   \\[\n   2\\alpha = \\alpha\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha = 0 \\text{ or } \\alpha = \\pi/2\n   \\]\n   Since \\(0 < \\alpha < \\pi\\), the only valid solution is:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha = \\pi/2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Finding \\(t\\):**\n   If \\(\\alpha = \\pi/2\\), then the sequence becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\arccos (\\cos (\\pi/2)), \\ \\arccos (\\cos (3\\pi/2)), \\ \\arccos (\\cos (11\\pi/2)), \\ \\arccos (\\cos (t\\pi/2))\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   0, \\ 0, \\ 0, \\ \\arccos (\\cos (t\\pi/2))\n   \\]\n   For the sequence to be an arithmetic progression, the third term must be \\(0\\), which implies:\n   \\[\n   \\arccos (\\cos (t\\pi/2)) = 0\n   \\]\n   This means:\n   \\[\n   \\cos (t\\pi/2) = 1\n   \\]\n   The smallest positive \\(t\\) that satisfies this is:\n   \\[\n   t = 1\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the least positive value of \\(t\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{17}\n\\]",
